<!-- Hosts (Computational Resources):
camera_host_1, camera_host_2, ... camera_host_N: Represents the camera's network interface or a minimal edge device right next to it.
Assign minimal processing power (e.g., 1 Gf - 1 Gigaflop/s), mainly acts as a starting point for data.

processing_node_1, processing_node_2, ... processing_node_M: These are the core workhorses (servers, potentially with GPUs) running OD and FR. 
Assign significant processing power (e.g., 10 Tf - 10 Teraflop/s, or more if simulating GPUs). 
M can be less than, equal to, or greater than N.

database_server: Hosts the personnel and access rule database. 
Needs moderate processing power for lookups (e.g., 100 Gf) and good I/O (SimGrid focuses on CPU/Network, less on disk I/O directly, but ensure network link is good).

alert_server: Receives and logs alerts. 
Can often be co-located with the database server or be a separate host. Minimal processing power needed (e.g., 10 Gf).

Links (Network Connections):
link_cam1_proc, link_cam2_proc, ...: Connect each camera_host_N to the network infrastructure that reaches the processing nodes. 
Define bandwidth (e.g., 1Gbps) and latency (e.g., 5ms). These might be slower links.

link_proc_db: Connects processing_node_M to database_server. 
Should be high bandwidth (e.g., 10Gbps) and low latency (e.g., 0.5ms), assuming a data center LAN.

link_proc_alert: Connects processing_node_M to alert_server. Similar to link_proc_db.

link_backbone (Optional but recommended): You might define a central switch or backbone link connecting processing nodes, DB server, and alert server, rather than direct links between all pairs.

Cluster/Zone (Optional): 
SimGrid allows grouping hosts into clusters, which can simplify defining network routes and properties within/between clusters (e.g., representing different buildings or network segments). -->

